          Affected versions of vllm are vulnerable to Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code           
          Injection') / Reachable Assertion. vLLM guards its activation-function loader (get_act_fn in        
          vllm/model_executor/layers/pooler/activations.py) with a single Python assert statement. When Python
          runs in optimized mode the assert is stripped at compile time, so an untrusted activation-function  
          name taken from a HuggingFace cross-encoder model configuration reaches an unrestricted import,     
          allowing an attacker who publishes a malicious model to achieve arbitrary code execution. Upgrade to
          vllm 0.22.0 or later.                                                                               
                                                                                                              
           ▶▶┆ Fixed for vllm at version: 0.22.0
          30348┆ [[package]]
   
   ❯❯❱ vllm - CVE-2026-24779
          Severity: HIGH                                                                                      
          Affected versions of vllm are vulnerable to Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF). An SSRF             
          vulnerability in vLLM's multimodal MediaConnector (load_from_url and load_from_url_async) arises    
          from inconsistent URL parsing: host validation uses urllib.urlparse (RFC 3986) while fetching uses  
          requests/urllib3 (WHATWG), and their differing handling of backslashes lets attackers bypass        
          allowed-host checks. An attacker can craft URLs with backslashes to force the server to make        
          arbitrary requests on internal networks, enabling port scanning, inter-pod interaction, DoS, or data
          exposure.                                                                                           
                                                                                                              
           ▶▶┆ Fixed for vllm at version: 0.14.1
